WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.800
A female common frog releases eggs into pond

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:06.360
water while a male releases sperm close by.

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:08.160
Now look at a hen's egg in a

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:08.720
nest.

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:12.080
In this example, it is already fertilised.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:14.640
Both eggs are outside the body when we

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:15.440
see them.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:17.520
But did sperm and egg join in the

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:18.480
same place?

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.520
To answer, we need to follow the reproductive

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:24.080
event, rather than classify an animal from where

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:25.280
its egg ends up.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:29.060
Recall from the previous lesson that sperm and

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:31.940
egg are reproductive cells called gametes.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:35.740
Each carries inherited information in DNA.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:38.260
When a sperm joins an egg, we call

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:39.700
that fertilisation.

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:41.940
It can begin the development of a new

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:42.500
animal.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:46.180
In this common-frog example, the female's eggs are

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:48.660
already in the pond when sperm reaches them.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:51.860
The joining happens in water outside her body.

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:54.660
That makes it external fertilisation.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:58.180
External describes the joining place, not whether the

00:00:58.260 --> 00:00:59.700
parents live in water.

00:01:00.934 --> 00:01:03.013
Why release the gametes close together?

00:01:03.654 --> 00:01:05.733
A sperm must reach an egg before it

00:01:05.814 --> 00:01:06.733
can join it.

00:01:07.014 --> 00:01:09.493
Nearby release shortens that journey.

00:01:09.814 --> 00:01:12.373
The water keeps these exposed gametes from drying

00:01:12.454 --> 00:01:14.933
out and provides liquid in which sperm can

00:01:15.014 --> 00:01:15.653
swim.

00:01:15.974 --> 00:01:19.013
But water alone does not guarantee a meeting.

00:01:19.094 --> 00:01:21.093
A current can carry sperm away from the

00:01:21.174 --> 00:01:21.973
eggs.

00:01:22.054 --> 00:01:23.653
If the two types of gamete are not

00:01:23.814 --> 00:01:25.733
present at the same time, they may miss

00:01:25.814 --> 00:01:27.093
each other too.

00:01:27.254 --> 00:01:30.693
So a suitable watery setting, a nearby release

00:01:30.814 --> 00:01:34.293
and overlapping timing support fertilisation in this case.

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:37.580
The hen uses a different route.

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:40.860
Sperm is transferred into the female during mating.

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:43.500
Inside her body, it can reach an egg

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:45.260
near the beginning of a tube called the

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:48.380
oviduct, the passage that carries the egg onwards.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.940
Joining there is internal fertilisation.

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:55.420
The moist reproductive tract reduces the gametes' exposure

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:57.740
to drying, which is useful on land.

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:00.300
That protection does not remove the need for

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:02.380
sperm to enter the female and reach an

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:02.780
egg.

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:05.900
The location gives a useful advantage under dry

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:06.700
conditions.

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:08.940
It does not promise that every egg will

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:12.060
be fertilised or that an offspring will survive.

00:02:13.267 --> 00:02:15.786
Return to the egg in our opening nest.

00:02:16.067 --> 00:02:18.786
In a hen, sperm reaches the egg before

00:02:18.867 --> 00:02:20.306
the hard shell is added.

00:02:20.627 --> 00:02:23.506
The egg then passes along the reproductive tract,

00:02:23.547 --> 00:02:25.506
the shell forms around it, and the hen

00:02:25.587 --> 00:02:26.426
lays it.

00:02:26.787 --> 00:02:29.586
In our supplied fertilised egg, the joining has

00:02:29.667 --> 00:02:32.066
already happened inside the body.

00:02:32.147 --> 00:02:34.466
Later development can continue in the laid egg

00:02:34.547 --> 00:02:36.386
when conditions support it.

00:02:36.427 --> 00:02:40.546
For this bird, remember the order: join, shell,

00:02:40.627 --> 00:02:41.586
lay.

00:02:41.707 --> 00:02:43.506
That order explains why an egg in a

00:02:43.707 --> 00:02:46.786
nest does not make fertilisation external.

00:02:46.907 --> 00:02:48.706
Find the joining place first.

00:02:49.934 --> 00:02:51.533
Try a changed condition.

00:02:52.254 --> 00:02:54.493
Two groups of common frogs release the same

00:02:54.574 --> 00:02:56.973
numbers of eggs and sperm into pond water

00:02:57.054 --> 00:02:58.293
at the same time.

00:02:58.574 --> 00:03:00.973
Their gametes remain able to fertilise for the

00:03:01.094 --> 00:03:02.333
same length of time.

00:03:02.654 --> 00:03:05.613
In group B, a stronger current carries sperm

00:03:05.694 --> 00:03:07.253
away from the egg patch.

00:03:07.534 --> 00:03:09.853
Classify the fertilisation in this route.

00:03:10.494 --> 00:03:13.053
Predict how B's opportunities for sperm to reach

00:03:13.214 --> 00:03:16.533
eggs compare with group A, and explain why.

00:03:16.894 --> 00:03:20.333
Then name one result these observations cannot tell

00:03:20.494 --> 00:03:20.933
us.

00:03:21.214 --> 00:03:22.573
Pause here to decide.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:38.200
The fertilisation is external because sperm and egg

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:40.840
join in pond water outside the female.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:44.680
For B, the stronger current carries sperm away

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:47.560
from the eggs, so sperm has fewer opportunities

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:49.640
to reach them in the stated comparison.

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:52.840
Equal starting numbers do not remove that extra

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:53.880
separation.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:57.400
The unchanged viable lifetime, the time gametes remain

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:00.240
able to fertilise, does not remove it either.

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:03.240
We cannot calculate an exact number of fertilised

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:04.920
eggs from these observations.

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:07.080
Nor can we predict how many young will

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:09.000
survive and later reproduce.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:12.520
The evidence supports a change in meeting opportunities,

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:15.000
rather than a guaranteed offspring count.

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:19.720
We used two animal examples to locate fertilisation

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:22.000
and explain a relevant advantage.

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:25.320
Common frogs join gametes outside in water.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:28.840
Our hen joins them inside before shell formation

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:29.560
and laying.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:33.080
Different routes still depend on suitable conditions.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:36.200
Next, we'll follow how a flower produces a

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:36.760
seed.

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:39.800
Detailed mammalian reproduction comes later.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:42.920
If you'd like a short set of notes,

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:45.640
copy these now, or pause for longer.
